{"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib"]]},"kind":"symbol","relationshipsSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/PointerWrapper","doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIProtocol"],"type":"conformsTo","kind":"relationships","title":"Conforms To"}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/glib\/uriref"]}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"sections":[],"abstract":[{"text":"The ","type":"text"},{"code":"GUri","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" type and related functions can be used to parse URIs into"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"their components, and build valid URIs from individual components.","type":"text"}],"topicSections":[{"title":"Initializers","generated":true,"anchor":"Initializers","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/init(_:)-1m0g3","doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/init(_:)-2ctpq","doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/init(_:)-4kx37","doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/init(_:)-5ugyq","doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/init(_:)-86fph","doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/init(cPointer:)","doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/init(constPointer:)","doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/init(gconstpointer:)","doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/init(gpointer:)","doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/init(mutating:)","doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/init(opaquePointer:)"]},{"anchor":"Instance-Properties","title":"Instance Properties","generated":true,"identifiers":["doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/ptr"]},{"generated":true,"anchor":"Type-Methods","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/build(flags:scheme:userinfo:host:port:path:query:fragment:)","doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/buildWith(user:scheme:user:password:authParams:host:port:path:query:fragment:)","doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/parse(uriString:flags:)"],"title":"Type Methods"},{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/PointerWrapper-Implementations","doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef\/URIProtocol-Implementations"],"title":"Default Implementations","generated":true,"anchor":"Default-Implementations"}],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"tokens":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"kind":"identifier","text":"URIRef"}],"platforms":["macOS"],"languages":["swift"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"kind":"content","content":[{"level":2,"anchor":"overview","type":"heading","text":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Since ","type":"text"},{"code":"GUri","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" only represents absolute URIs, all ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GUri"},{"type":"text","text":"s will have a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"URI scheme, so [method","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.URI.get_scheme"},{"type":"text","text":"] will always return a non-"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"NULL"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"answer. Likewise, by definition, all URIs have a path component, so"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"[method"},{"code":"GLib.URI.get_path","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"] will always return a non-"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"NULL"},{"text":" string (which may","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"be empty)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"If the URI string has an"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"https:\/\/tools.ietf.org\/html\/rfc3986#section-3"},{"text":" (that","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"is, if the scheme is followed by "},{"code":":\/\/","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" rather than just "},{"code":":","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"), then the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"GUri","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" will contain a hostname, and possibly a port and ‘userinfo’.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Additionally, depending on how the ","type":"text"},{"code":"GUri","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" was constructed\/parsed (for example,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"using the "},{"code":"G_URI_FLAGS_HAS_PASSWORD","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"code":"G_URI_FLAGS_HAS_AUTH_PARAMS","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" flags),","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the userinfo may be split out into a username, password, and"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"additional authorization-related parameters.","type":"text"}]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Normally, the components of a ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GUri"},{"text":" will have all ","type":"text"},{"code":"-encoded characters decoded. However, if you construct\/parse a `GUri` with `G_URI_FLAGS_ENCODED`, then the ","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"-encoding will be preserved instead in"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the userinfo, path, and query fields (and in the host field if also","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"created with ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"G_URI_FLAGS_NON_DNS"},{"text":"). In particular, this is necessary if","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the URI may contain binary data or non-UTF-8 text, or if decoding"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"the components might change the interpretation of the URI."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"For example, with the encoded flag:"}]},{"type":"codeListing","code":["`g_autoptr(GUri)` uri = g_uri_parse (\"http:\/\/host\/path?query=http`3A``2F``2Fhost``2Fpath``3Fparam``3Dvalue`\", G_URI_FLAGS_ENCODED, &err);","g_assert_cmpstr (g_uri_get_query (uri), ==, \"query=http`3A``2F``2Fhost``2Fpath``3Fparam``3Dvalue`\");"],"syntax":"c"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"While the default ````-decoding behaviour would give:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"c","code":["`g_autoptr(GUri)` uri = g_uri_parse (\"http:\/\/host\/path?query=http`3A``2F``2Fhost``2Fpath``3Fparam``3Dvalue`\", G_URI_FLAGS_NONE, &err);","g_assert_cmpstr (g_uri_get_query (uri), ==, \"query=http:\/\/host\/path?param=value\");"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"During decoding, if an invalid UTF-8 string is encountered, parsing will fail","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"with an error indicating the bad string location:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"codeListing","syntax":"c","code":["`g_autoptr(GUri)` uri = g_uri_parse (\"http:\/\/host\/path?query=http`3A``2F``2Fhost``2Fpath``3Fbad``3D``00alue`\", G_URI_FLAGS_NONE, &err);","g_assert_error (err, G_URI_ERROR, G_URI_ERROR_BAD_QUERY);"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"You should pass ","type":"text"},{"code":"G_URI_FLAGS_ENCODED","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"G_URI_FLAGS_ENCODED_QUERY"},{"type":"text","text":" if you"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"need to handle that case manually. In particular, if the query string"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"contains ","type":"text"},{"code":"=","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" characters that are ````-encoded, you should let","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"[func"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.URI.parse_params"},{"type":"text","text":"] do the decoding once of the query."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":"GUri","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is immutable once constructed, and can safely be accessed from","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"multiple threads. Its reference counting is atomic.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Note that the scope of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GUri"},{"text":" is to help manipulate URIs in various applications,","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"following "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"https:\/\/tools.ietf.org\/html\/rfc3986"},{"text":". In particular,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"it doesn’t intend to cover web browser needs, and doesn’t implement the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"https:\/\/url.spec.whatwg.org\/"},{"type":"text","text":" standard. No APIs are provided to"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"help prevent"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"https:\/\/en.wikipedia.org\/wiki\/IDN_homograph_attack","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"text":", so","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"GUri","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is not suitable for formatting URIs for display to the user for making"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"security-sensitive decisions."}]},{"text":"Relative and absolute URIs","level":2,"anchor":"Relative-and-absolute-URIs","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"As defined in "},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"https:\/\/tools.ietf.org\/html\/rfc3986#section-4","type":"reference"},{"text":", the","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"hierarchical nature of URIs means that they can either be ‘relative","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"references’ (sometimes referred to as ‘relative URIs’) or ‘URIs’ (for"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"clarity, ‘URIs’ are referred to in this documentation as"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"‘absolute URIs’ — although"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"https:\/\/tools.ietf.org\/html\/rfc3986#section-4.3"},{"type":"text","text":","},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"fragment identifiers are always allowed).","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Relative references have one or more components of the URI missing. In"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"particular, they have no scheme. Any other component, such as hostname,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"query, etc. may be missing, apart from a path, which has to be specified (but"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"may be empty). The path may be relative, starting with "},{"code":".\/","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" rather than "},{"code":"\/","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"For example, a valid relative reference is "},{"code":".\/path?query","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":",","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"\/?query"},{"type":"text","text":"fragment`` or "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"\/\/example.com"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Absolute URIs have a scheme specified. Any other components of the URI which","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"are missing are specified as explicitly unset in the URI, rather than being"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"resolved relative to a base URI using [method"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.URI.parse_relative"},{"text":"].","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"For example, a valid absolute URI is ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"file:\/\/\/home\/bob"},{"text":" or","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"https:\/\/search.com?query=string","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"A "},{"code":"GUri","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" instance is always an absolute URI. A string may be an absolute URI"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"or a relative reference; see the documentation for individual functions as to"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"what forms they accept."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"text":"Parsing URIs","type":"heading","anchor":"Parsing-URIs"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The most minimalist APIs for parsing URIs are [func","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.URI.split"},{"text":"] and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"[func","type":"text"},{"code":"GLib.URI.split_with_user","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"]. These split a URI into its component"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"parts, and return the parts; the difference between the two is that"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"[func","type":"text"},{"code":"GLib.URI.split","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"] treats the ‘userinfo’ component of the URI as a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"single element, while [func","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.URI.split_with_user"},{"type":"text","text":"] can (depending on the"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"[flags"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIFlags"},{"type":"text","text":"] you pass) treat it as containing a username, password,"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"and authentication parameters. Alternatively, [func","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.URI.split_network"},{"type":"text","text":"]"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"can be used when you are only interested in the components that are"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"needed to initiate a network connection to the service (scheme,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"host, and port)."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"[func","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.URI.parse"},{"type":"text","text":"] is similar to [func"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.URI.split"},{"type":"text","text":"], but instead of"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"returning individual strings, it returns a ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GUri"},{"text":" structure (and it requires","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"that the URI be an absolute URI)."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"[func","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.URI.resolve_relative"},{"type":"text","text":"] and [method"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.URI.parse_relative"},{"type":"text","text":"] allow"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"you to resolve a relative URI relative to a base URI."},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"[func","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.URI.resolve_relative"},{"text":"] takes two strings and returns a string,","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"and [method"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.URI.parse_relative"},{"type":"text","text":"] takes a "},{"code":"GUri","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and a string and returns a","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GUri"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"All of the parsing functions take a [flags"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIFlags","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":"] argument describing","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"exactly how to parse the URI; see the documentation for that type"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"for more details on the specific flags that you can pass. If you","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"need to choose different flags based on the type of URI, you can","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"use [func"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.URI.peek_scheme"},{"type":"text","text":"] on the URI string to check the scheme"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"first, and use that to decide what flags to parse it with."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"For example, you might want to use ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"G_URI_PARAMS_WWW_FORM"},{"text":" when parsing the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"params for a web URI, so compare the result of [func","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.URI.peek_scheme"},{"text":"]","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"against ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"http"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"code":"https","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"text":"Building URIs","level":2,"anchor":"Building-URIs","type":"heading"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"[func","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.URI.join"},{"text":"] and [func","type":"text"},{"code":"GLib.URI.join_with_user","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"] can be used to construct"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"valid URI strings from a set of component strings. They are the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"inverse of [func","type":"text"},{"code":"GLib.URI.split","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"] and [func"},{"code":"GLib.URI.split_with_user","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"]."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Similarly, [func","type":"text"},{"code":"GLib.URI.build","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"] and [func","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.URI.build_with_user"},{"text":"] can be","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"used to construct a "},{"code":"GUri","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" from a set of component strings.","type":"text"}]},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"As with the parsing functions, the building functions take a","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"[flags"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIFlags","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":"] argument. In particular, it is important to keep in mind","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"whether the URI components you are using are already ````-encoded. If so,","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"you must pass the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"G_URI_FLAGS_ENCODED"},{"type":"text","text":" flag."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"heading","text":"`file:\/\/` URIs","anchor":"file-URIs","level":2},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Note that Windows and Unix both define special rules for parsing"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"file:\/\/"},{"text":" URIs (involving non-UTF-8 character sets on Unix, and the","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"interpretation of path separators on Windows). "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GUri"},{"type":"text","text":" does not"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":"implement these rules. Use [func"},{"code":"GLib.filename_from_uri","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"] and","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"[func"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GLib.filename_to_uri"},{"text":"] if you want to properly convert between","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"file:\/\/","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" URIs and local filenames."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"text":"URI Equality","anchor":"URI-Equality","type":"heading"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Note that there is no "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"g_uri_equal ()"},{"text":" function, because comparing","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"URIs usefully requires scheme-specific knowledge that ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GUri"},{"text":" does","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"not have. "},{"code":"GUri","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" can help with normalization if you use the various","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"encoded [flags","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIFlags","type":"reference"},{"text":"] as well as ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"G_URI_FLAGS_SCHEME_NORMALIZE"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"text":"however it is not comprehensive.","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"For example, "},{"code":"data:,foo","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"code":"data:;base64,Zm9v","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" resolve to the same","type":"text"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"thing according to the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"data:"},{"type":"text","text":" URI specification which GLib does not"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"handle.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The "},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef"},{"type":"text","text":" type acts as a lightweight Swift reference to an underlying "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GUri"},{"text":" instance.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"It exposes methods that can operate on this data type through ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIProtocol","isActive":true},{"text":" conformance.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"Use ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URIRef","type":"reference"},{"type":"text","text":" only as an "},{"code":"unowned","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" reference to an existing "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"GUri"},{"text":" instance.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"name":"Note","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Use "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/GLib\/documentation\/GLib\/URI"},{"text":" (instead of ","type